        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Expect ten to fifteen years from a garage door opener, and expect the door's springs and balance to decide which end of that range you reach. Most of the failures along the way, gears, sensors, belts and capacitors, are cheap enough to fix on an opener that is still young.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Age changes the answer in two ways. Past fifteen years, repairs rarely pay off against a new unit with better security and battery backup. And any opener without photo-eye sensors should be replaced now, whether it runs perfectly or not.
          </p>
